# UI Style Guide

Conventions for anything a user sees or touches. These are not preferences; a
change that breaks one of them is a bug.

## Interaction states appear instantly and fade out gently

**Never put a transition on the way IN to a hover or focus state. Only on the
way out.**

A fade-in on hover reads as a slow UI. The pointer is already there and the
interface is still catching up, and no duration is short enough to feel
deliberate — it just feels like lag. The way out is different: the pointer has
left, nobody is waiting on it, and a gentle fade keeps the change from snapping.

The mechanism is that an element transitions using the `transition` declared on
the state it is moving **to**. So the two directions live in two different rules,
and which rule owns which direction is the opposite of most people's first
guess:

- The **base rule** is the state you return to, so it owns **hover-OFF**. Put the
  gentle duration here.
- The **state rule** (`:hover`, `:focus-visible`) is the state you move to, so it
  owns **hover-ON**. Set `transition-duration: 0s` here.

Worked example, the footer link underline:

```css
.job-footer__link {
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-decoration-color: transparent;
  /* Hover-OFF only: gentle on the way out. */
  transition: text-decoration-color 150ms ease;
}

.job-footer__link:hover,
.job-footer__link:focus-visible {
  text-decoration-color: var(--ink);
  /* Hover-ON: instant, never a fade. */
  transition-duration: 0s;
}
```

Applies to every interaction state — colour, underline, background, border,
opacity, outline, transform. Pair `:hover` with `:focus-visible` on the same rule
so a keyboard reaches every state the mouse does.

Two things this rule does **not** cover: entrance animations for content arriving
on the page, and state that takes real time (a pending request). Those are not
interaction states.

## Sentence case for all titles

**Every title is sentence case. Not Title Case.**

This covers all of it — page headings, section headings, buttons, labels, nav
items, link text, form legends, table headers, empty states, error messages,
tooltips, `aria-label`s, page `<title>`s, and metadata.

Capitalise the first word. Capitalise nothing else unless it is a proper noun.

| Write this | Not this |
| --- | --- |
| Apply for this job | Apply For This Job |
| About us | About Us |
| Do not sell or share my personal information | Do Not Sell Or Share My Personal Information |
| Netflix House jobs | Netflix House Jobs |
| Posted date | Posted Date |

Proper nouns keep their capitals: Netflix, Netflix House, Netflix Jobs. In
"Netflix House jobs" the venue is the proper noun and "jobs" is an ordinary
plural, so only the name is capitalised.

Acronyms stay uppercase (US, FAQ, API). Sentence case is about capitalisation
only — it does not add terminal punctuation, so headings and buttons still take
no full stop.

When mirroring a third party's copy, the third party's Title Case is not a
reason to keep it. Match their wording, use our capitalisation.
